; Where ns.sibling. really belongs.  This zone is the one place with any
; standing to say what address that host has -- and it says the host has
; none.  The name exists here and has other records, so the answer is a
; signed "no such data" rather than "no such name".
;
; The only address for ns.sibling. anywhere, then, is the one the root
; hands out as glue.  Whether a resolver may pass that on to a client
; as though the sibling zone had said it is the question tkix.net asks:
; "Sibling domain の glue は信じて良いのでしょうか?".
$ORIGIN sibling.
$TTL 3600
@	IN	SOA	ns.sibling. root.sibling. 1 3600 900 604800 3600
@	IN	NS	ns.sibling.
ns	IN	TXT	"the address of this host is nowhere in this zone"
